if you had a 256 gb phone with just the phone things needed and the rest filled with ebooks...could you read all of those books in a lifetime?

---

No, not even close—you'd have tens of thousands of books, but a human lifetime only allows for a few thousand at most (often far fewer).
Storage Capacity
A 256 GB phone would leave roughly 200–230 GB for ebooks after accounting for the OS, pre-installed apps, and essential "phone things" (typically 15–30+ GB, plus some buffer for updates and overhead).
Average ebook size is around 2–3 MB for a typical novel (e.g., ~300 pages or 75,000 words in EPUB/AZW3 format). Heavier books with images or PDFs can be much larger, but plain text novels are often under 1–2 MB.

At ~2.6 MB per book: You'd fit roughly 80,000–90,000 books on the available space.
Even conservatively (say 5 MB average with some variety), you'd still get 40,000+ books.

That's a massive personal library—equivalent to multiple large public library sections.
Human Reading Capacity in a Lifetime
Realistically, even a dedicated reader won't come close:

Average reader: ~12 books/year. Over ~50–60 remaining adult years (depending on age/lifespan), that's roughly 600–800 books total.
Voracious reader: 50 books/year → maybe 2,500–3,000 in a lifetime.
Super reader: 80+ books/year (very rare, full-time commitment) → up to ~4,000–5,000.

Some estimates put a typical lifetime total around 1,000–2,000 books for enthusiastic readers.
Factors limiting this:

Reading speed (most people average 200–300 words/minute for pleasure reading).
Daily time available (even 1–2 hours/day is a lot for many).
Life obligations, sleep, work, etc.
Comprehension and retention—skimming doesn't count as "reading" in the full sense.

Bottom Line
You could store 10–100x more books than even the fastest, most dedicated reader could finish in a full lifetime. The phone would hold an entire life's worth (or several lifetimes') of reading material with room to spare, but you'd only ever get through a small fraction. The real bottleneck is time and attention, not storage.
It's a great thought experiment for why curation (picking the right books) matters more than sheer quantity! If you read heavily, you might finish a few thousand over decades, but the rest would be digital heirlooms for... well, probably never.</span>